-- Q10 [Cell Theory & Prokaryotic Fundamentals · hard · Skill 2 · ans A] (g55)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Cell Theory & Prokaryotic Fundamentals',
    $qt55$A clinician treats a bacterial infection with an aminoglycoside, which binds the 30S subunit of the patient's bacterial cells and blocks their protein synthesis. The patient's own cytoplasmic translation continues largely unaffected. Which feature of the patient's cytoplasmic ribosomes BEST explains why this drug spares them?$qt55$,
    $op55$[{"label":"A","text":"They are 80S ribosomes built from 40S and 60S subunits, so they lack the 30S subunit the drug binds."},{"label":"B","text":"They are 70S ribosomes built from 40S and 60S subunits, so the drug cannot reach their small subunit."},{"label":"C","text":"They are 80S ribosomes built from 30S and 50S subunits, but the drug binds only free 30S subunits."},{"label":"D","text":"They are 70S ribosomes built from 30S and 50S subunits whose binding site for the drug is masked by rRNA."}]$op55$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex55$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is A because the patient's cytoplasmic ribosomes are eukaryotic 80S ribosomes assembled from 40S and 60S subunits, and they therefore have no 30S subunit for the drug to bind. The drug's target — the 30S subunit — exists only in the bacterial 70S ribosome (30S + 50S), so the antibiotic shuts down bacterial translation while leaving the host's structurally distinct cytoplasmic machinery intact. The text states that "Prokaryotic ribosomes... are called 70S ribosomes" whereas "eukaryotic cytoplasmic ribosomes have a size of 80S," and that the prokaryotic ribosome's "small subunit is described as 30S... and the large subunit is 50S... for a total of 70S," while "Eukaryote ribosomes have a small 40S subunit... and a large 60S subunit... for a total of 80S" (openstax-microbiology.txt, lines 4470-4471, 18769-18772). Because the 30S subunit is a prokaryote-specific component, a 30S-binding drug discriminates between the two cell types. (Choice A) Correct: the host cytoplasmic ribosome is 80S (40S + 60S) and simply has no 30S subunit, so the drug has nothing to bind. (Choice B) Wrong: it gives the right subunits (40S/60S) but the wrong size — eukaryotic cytoplasmic ribosomes are 80S, not 70S; the "70S" label belongs to prokaryotes, and the sparing is due to the missing 30S subunit, not poor drug access. (Choice C) Wrong: it reverses the subunit assignment — 30S and 50S are the bacterial 70S subunits, not the eukaryotic 80S subunits, so this describes a ribosome the drug WOULD target. (Choice D) Wrong: a 70S ribosome made of 30S + 50S is exactly the bacterial ribosome the drug binds, so this would be impaired, not spared. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must combine the drug's molecular target (the 30S subunit) with the structural difference between 70S and 80S ribosomes to predict which cell type is selectively affected.$ex55$,
    'hard', '2B', $cc55$Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)$cc55$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'scale_unit_error', $a55B$Correct subunits (40S/60S) but mislabels the eukaryotic ribosome as 70S instead of 80S, and substitutes a wrong mechanism (drug access)$a55B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a55C$Calls the host ribosome 80S but assigns it the prokaryotic 30S/50S subunits, swapping which subunits belong to which ribosome$a55C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a55D$Describes the actual bacterial 70S (30S+50S) target as if it were the spared host ribosome, the classic eukaryote/prokaryote mixup$a55D$ FROM q;

-- Q11 [Prokaryotic Cell Wall & Gram Staining · easy · Skill 1 · ans C] (g61)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Prokaryotic Cell Wall & Gram Staining',
    $qt61$A microbiologist strips away a bacterium's plasma membrane and isolates the rigid mesh that lay just outside it. Chemical analysis shows the mesh is a polymer of alternating sugars cross-linked by short peptide chains, and the same material is present in essentially all bacteria yet absent from the patient's own cells. The isolated macromolecule is most directly identified as:$qt61$,
    $op61$[{"label":"A","text":"Pseudopeptidoglycan, the sugar-and-peptide wall polymer that surrounds the cells of the domain Archaea"},{"label":"B","text":"Cellulose, a polymer of glucose units that forms the rigid wall surrounding plant and algal cells"},{"label":"C","text":"Peptidoglycan, the sugar polymer cross-linked by short peptides that forms the wall of essentially all bacteria"},{"label":"D","text":"A phospholipid bilayer, the fluid sheet of lipid molecules that encloses the bacterial cytoplasm"}]$op61$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex61$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is C because peptidoglycan is the sugar-peptide polymer that builds the rigid cell wall of essentially all bacteria and is absent from human cells. The reference states, "Most bacteria have cell walls that contain peptidoglycan" (openstax-microbiology.txt, lines 1632–1633), and the Gram-stain mechanism describes the "thick layers of peptidoglycan in the cell walls" that trap crystal violet (lines 3182–3186), confirming peptidoglycan as the wall macromolecule built from sugars cross-linked by short peptides. Because human cells lack this polymer entirely, peptidoglycan synthesis is a classic selective antibiotic target. (Choice A) Pseudopeptidoglycan is a real wall polymer, but it surrounds Archaea, not Bacteria: "archaeal cell walls do not contain peptidoglycan, but their cell walls are often composed of a similar substance called pseudopeptidoglycan" (lines 1656–1657), so it is the wrong domain for a bacterium. (Choice B) Cellulose is a glucose polymer that forms plant and algal walls and lacks the peptide cross-links described, so it does not match a polymer "cross-linked by short peptides" in a bacterium. (Choice C) Peptidoglycan is the sugar polymer cross-linked by short peptides that forms the bacterial wall and is absent from human cells. (Choice D) A phospholipid bilayer is the plasma membrane that the microbiologist already stripped away; it is a fluid lipid sheet, not the rigid polymeric mesh that lay outside it. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the chemical identity of the bacterial cell-wall macromolecule.$ex61$,
    'easy', '2B', $cc61$Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)$cc61$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'adjacent_fact', $a61A$Archaeal analog (pseudopeptidoglycan) swapped for the bacterial polymer$a61A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a61B$Eukaryotic wall polysaccharide (cellulose) mistaken for the bacterial wall$a61B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a61D$Plasma membrane (lipid bilayer) confused with the rigid wall outside it$a61D$ FROM q;

-- Q12 [Prokaryotic Cell Wall & Gram Staining · medium · Skill 2 · ans D] (g62)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Prokaryotic Cell Wall & Gram Staining',
    $qt62$A clinical microbiology lab characterizes an unknown bacterium and reports that its cell envelope is a thick peptidoglycan layer studded with lipoteichoic acid and lacking any outer membrane. A technician then runs a standard Gram stain on the isolate—crystal violet, Gram's iodine, an alcohol decolorizer, and a safranin counterstain, in that order—and views the smear under the microscope. Based on the reported wall structure, what color do the cells MOST likely appear?$qt62$,
    $op62$[{"label":"A","text":"Pink, because the alcohol decolorizer washes the crystal violet out of the wall and the safranin counterstain then colors the cells"},{"label":"B","text":"Colorless, because a wall built from peptidoglycan binds neither the crystal violet nor the safranin and the cells take up no dye"},{"label":"C","text":"Both purple and pink, because the safranin counterstain overlays the retained crystal violet so the cells take on a mixed color"},{"label":"D","text":"Purple, because the thick peptidoglycan traps the crystal violet–iodine complex and resists decolorization, so the safranin is not visible"}]$op62$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex62$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is D because a thick peptidoglycan layer with teichoic/lipoteichoic acid and no outer membrane is the defining wall of a gram-positive cell, which retains the crystal violet–iodine complex through decolorization and therefore appears purple. The text states that "Gram-positive cells have a cell wall consisting of many layers of peptidoglycan totaling 30–100 nm in thickness" and that these "peptidoglycan layers are commonly embedded with teichoic acids (TAs)," whereas the more complex gram-negative envelope is defined by "a thin layer of peptidoglycan and an outer membrane containing lipopolysaccharide." Mechanistically, the iodine mordant makes "the crystal violet–iodine complex clump and stay contained in thick layers of peptidoglycan," and during decolorization "cells that have thick peptidoglycan layers in their cell walls... generally retain the crystal violet dye and remain purple," so the later safranin counterstain "is less noticeable in the cells that still contain the crystal violet dye." Because the described wall is thick peptidoglycan with lipoteichoic acid and no outer membrane, the cells are gram-positive and appear purple. (Choice A) is the gram-negative outcome reversed onto this cell: the decolorizer "more easily washes the dye out of cells with thinner peptidoglycan layers," not thick ones, so a thick-walled cell stays purple rather than turning pink. (Choice B) is wrong because peptidoglycan does bind dye—gram-positive walls retain crystal violet specifically because of their thickness—so the cells are not colorless. (Choice C) misreads the counterstain step: safranin "is less noticeable in the cells that still contain the crystal violet dye," so a gram-positive cell reads as a single purple color, not a blended purple-and-pink. (Choice D) is correct because the trapped crystal violet–iodine complex survives decolorization in thick peptidoglycan and masks the safranin, yielding purple cells. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must take the described wall structure, map it to the gram-positive category, and chain the decolorization and counterstain steps to predict the observed color.$ex62$,
    'medium', '2B', $cc62$Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)$cc62$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a62A$Applies the gram-negative (thin-wall, decolorized, safranin-pink) outcome to a thick-walled cell, inverting the decolorization rule$a62A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a62B$Believes a peptidoglycan wall binds no stain, so the cell stays colorless regardless of thickness$a62B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a62C$Correctly notes crystal violet is retained but wrongly adds the safranin color on top, predicting a mixed purple-pink instead of masked pink$a62C$ FROM q;

-- Q13 [Prokaryotic Cell Wall & Gram Staining · medium · Skill 2 · ans A] (g63)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Prokaryotic Cell Wall & Gram Staining',
    $qt63$A clinical isolate has only a thin (~4 nm) peptidoglycan layer enclosed by an outer membrane, with a periplasmic space between the two. After the crystal-violet and iodine steps of a Gram stain, the smear is flushed with ethanol and the cells finish pink. Which mechanism best accounts for the loss of the crystal-violet/iodine complex from these cells during decolorization?$qt63$,
    $op63$[{"label":"A","text":"Ethanol dissolves the lipid-rich outer membrane, and the thin peptidoglycan layer left behind cannot retain the trapped crystal-violet/iodine complex, so the dye washes out."},{"label":"B","text":"Ethanol dehydrates and shrinks the thick peptidoglycan layer, closing its pores so the crystal-violet/iodine complex is locked inside and cannot escape."},{"label":"C","text":"Ethanol chemically breaks the bond between crystal violet and iodine, and only the freed iodine is small enough to diffuse out through the intact outer membrane."},{"label":"D","text":"Ethanol strips the lipopolysaccharide endotoxin from the outer leaflet, and loss of this negatively charged layer prevents the cell from binding the positively charged crystal violet in the first place."}]$op63$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex63$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is A because in a gram-negative cell the alcohol decolorizer dissolves the lipid outer membrane, and the underlying thin peptidoglycan layer is too sparse to hold the trapped crystal-violet/iodine complex, so the dye washes out and the cell later takes up the pink safranin counterstain. The text describes the gram-negative envelope as having "a much thinner layer of peptidoglycan (no more than about 4 nm thick) than gram-positive cells," with "a second lipid bilayer called the outer membrane" external to that peptidoglycan (lines 4784-4787). Gram's iodine works by forming a complex that stays "contained in thick layers of peptidoglycan," and the decolorizer "more easily washes the dye out of cells with thinner peptidoglycan layers, making them again colorless" (lines 3181-3186). Thus retention depends on a thick peptidoglycan mesh trapping the complex; a thin layer, once its protective outer membrane is dissolved by the lipid-solubilizing alcohol, simply cannot hold the dye. (Choice A) Correct: the alcohol solubilizes the outer-membrane lipids and the thin peptidoglycan fails to retain the crystal-violet/iodine complex, matching the text's statement that decolorizer washes dye from thin-peptidoglycan cells. (Choice B) Reversed: this describes the gram-positive (purple-retaining) outcome, where a thick peptidoglycan layer dehydrates and traps the complex; the stem's cell has a thin layer and ends up pink. (Choice C) The mordant action of iodine stabilizes the complex inside the wall; alcohol does not cleave a crystal-violet–iodine bond and let iodine alone escape, so this misstates the decolorization mechanism. (Choice D) The pink result reflects loss of an already-bound complex during decolorization, not a failure to bind crystal violet initially; crystal violet stains all cells purple before the iodine and alcohol steps, so LPS charge does not prevent initial uptake. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must combine the cell's structural features with the staining mechanism to predict why the dye is lost.$ex63$,
    'medium', '2B', $cc63$Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)$cc63$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a63B$Applies the gram-positive retention mechanism (thick peptidoglycan dehydrates and traps dye) to a gram-negative cell$a63B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a63C$Imagines alcohol chemically cleaves the crystal-violet/iodine complex so iodine diffuses out, misunderstanding the mordant's role$a63C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', $a63D$Uses a true fact about LPS charge/endotoxin to claim crystal violet never bound, confusing initial staining with decolorization loss$a63D$ FROM q;

-- Q14 [Prokaryotic Cell Wall & Gram Staining · hard · Skill 2 · ans B] (g64)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Prokaryotic Cell Wall & Gram Staining',
    $qt64$A patient develops high fever and circulatory shock during a bloodstream infection by a pink-staining bacillus. When a cell-wall-active antibiotic is started, the systemic inflammatory reaction abruptly worsens, and a researcher isolates a heat-stable component shed from the bacterium's outer membrane as the trigger. Which molecule is responsible, and why does its release drive the septic response?$qt64$,
    $op64$[{"label":"A","text":"A secreted protein exotoxin, because antibiotic killing causes the bacterium to release its actively secreted toxins into the blood, where they directly damage host tissue"},{"label":"B","text":"Lipopolysaccharide (LPS), because its lipid A moiety in the outer membrane is a heat-stable endotoxin that is liberated when the cell lyses and provokes fever and septic shock"},{"label":"C","text":"Teichoic acid, because this outer-membrane polymer is shed during lysis and is the heat-stable endotoxin that elicits a systemic inflammatory response"},{"label":"D","text":"Peptidoglycan, because the thick murein layer of this organism is the heat-stable outer-membrane endotoxin that fragments during lysis to drive shock"}]$op64$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex64$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is B because a pink (safranin-counterstained) result identifies a gram-negative cell, whose distinguishing feature is an outer membrane whose outer leaflet carries lipopolysaccharide (LPS); LPS is a heat-stable endotoxin, and its lipid A anchor is the toxic principle that, when liberated by lysis, triggers fever and septic shock. The text states that the outer membrane's outer leaflet "contains the molecule lipopolysaccharide (LPS), which functions as an endotoxin in infections involving gram-negative bacteria, contributing to symptoms such as fever, hemorrhaging, and septic shock," and that "Each LPS molecule is composed of Lipid A, a core polysaccharide, and an O side chain" (openstax-microbiology.txt, lines 4787-4791). Because LPS is a structural membrane component rather than a secreted product, killing and lysing the bacterium with a cell-wall-active antibiotic releases a burst of LPS, which is exactly why antibiotic-induced lysis can transiently intensify the endotoxic reaction. The heat stability of the response is the classic hallmark distinguishing endotoxin (LPS) from heat-labile protein exotoxins. (Choice A) An exotoxin is an actively secreted protein toxin; it can cause shock, but it is heat-labile and is not a heat-stable component shed from the outer membrane, so it does not match the researcher's finding. (Choice B) is correct: the outer-membrane LPS, via its lipid A endotoxin, is heat-stable and is released by lysis to drive the septic response (lines 4788-4791). (Choice C) Teichoic acid is a real cell-wall polymer, but the text places it in gram-positive walls, "commonly embedded" in thick peptidoglycan, not in the gram-negative outer membrane, and it is not the endotoxin (lines 4745-4749). (Choice D) Peptidoglycan is present (a thin ~4 nm layer in gram-negative cells) and is structural, but the text identifies LPS — not peptidoglycan — as the outer-membrane endotoxin; peptidoglycan is the murein mesh, not a lipid-anchored membrane endotoxin (lines 4784-4789). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate the Gram-stain result, the gram-negative envelope architecture, and the consequences of antibiotic-induced lysis to predict which molecule drives the endotoxic response.$ex64$,
    'hard', '2B', $cc64$Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)$cc64$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'partial_truth', $a64A$Exotoxin also causes shock, but it is a heat-labile secreted protein, not a heat-stable membrane component released by lysis (510-tempting trap)$a64A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a64C$Teichoic acid is a real wall polymer but belongs to gram-positive peptidoglycan, not the gram-negative outer membrane, and is not the endotoxin$a64C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a64D$Peptidoglycan is present but is the structural murein mesh, not the lipid-anchored outer-membrane endotoxin$a64D$ FROM q;

-- Q15 [Prokaryotic Cell Wall & Gram Staining · hard · Skill 2 · ans C] (g65)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Prokaryotic Cell Wall & Gram Staining',
    $qt65$A microbiologist adds an enzyme that hydrolyzes peptidoglycan to two bacterial cultures held in isotonic buffer: one is a purple-staining species, and the other is a pink-staining species whose thin peptidoglycan layer lies beneath an intact outer membrane. The enzyme is applied without any agent that permeabilizes outer membranes. Which culture's cells are predicted to lyse more readily, and why?$qt65$,
    $op65$[{"label":"A","text":"The pink-staining culture, because its peptidoglycan is thinner and therefore offers less material for the enzyme to digest before the wall fails."},{"label":"B","text":"The purple-staining culture, because the crystal violet–iodine complex retained in its wall chemically activates the peptidoglycan-hydrolyzing enzyme."},{"label":"C","text":"The purple-staining culture, because its thick peptidoglycan is directly exposed at the cell surface, whereas the pink-staining cells' outer membrane shields their peptidoglycan from the enzyme."},{"label":"D","text":"The pink-staining culture, because its outer membrane is itself the enzyme's primary substrate and is degraded before the underlying peptidoglycan."}]$op65$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex65$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is C because the enzyme can only lyse a cell if it can physically reach and hydrolyze the peptidoglycan, and only the purple-staining (gram-positive) cell exposes its peptidoglycan directly at the surface. Purple-staining cells are gram-positive, with a thick peptidoglycan wall (30–100 nm) external to the plasma membrane and no covering outer membrane, so a peptidoglycan-hydrolyzing enzyme such as lysozyme contacts its substrate directly: the text notes lysozyme "breaks down and digests the peptidoglycan in their cell walls." Pink-staining cells are gram-negative, where "a second lipid bilayer called the outer membrane … is external to the peptidoglycan layer"; this outer membrane is a barrier the enzyme cannot cross, so the thin peptidoglycan beneath it is protected and the cell resists lysis. The trap is the intuition that less peptidoglycan means easier lysis, but accessibility, not wall thickness, governs the outcome here. (Choice A) This reverses the actual cause: although the gram-negative wall is thinner, its peptidoglycan is sealed off beneath the intact outer membrane, so the enzyme never reaches it; thinness does not help when access is blocked. (Choice B) The crystal violet–iodine complex is a staining artifact that is trapped in thick peptidoglycan during the Gram procedure; it has no role in catalyzing or activating a hydrolytic enzyme, so this misattributes the mechanism. (Choice C) Correct: the gram-positive wall's thick peptidoglycan is surface-exposed and directly accessible, while the gram-negative outer membrane shields its peptidoglycan from the enzyme. (Choice D) The enzyme is specific for peptidoglycan, not for the lipid-and-LPS outer membrane; without a permeabilizing agent the outer membrane stays intact and continues to exclude the enzyme, so it is not degraded first. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must combine the gram-positive and gram-negative envelope architectures with the enzyme's substrate specificity to predict which cell is more readily lysed, rather than recalling a single fact.$ex65$,
    'hard', '2B', $cc65$Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)$cc65$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a65A$Treats thinner peptidoglycan as inherently easier to lyse, ignoring that the outer membrane blocks enzyme access$a65A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a65B$Confuses a Gram-stain dye artifact (crystal violet–iodine complex) with a biochemical activator of the enzyme$a65B$ FROM q
