-- Q16 [Prokaryotic Cell Wall & Gram Staining · hard · Skill 2 · ans D] (g66)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Prokaryotic Cell Wall & Gram Staining',
    $qt66$A student Gram-stains a known gram-positive control three times, changing only the duration of the ethanol decolorization step and recording the final color seen under the microscope: \(\begin{array}{lc} \text{Decolorization time} & \text{Observed color} \\ 10\ \text{s} & \text{purple} \\ 30\ \text{s} & \text{purple} \\ 90\ \text{s} & \text{pink} \end{array}\) The control organism is genuinely gram-positive in every trial. Which inference BEST explains the pink result at 90 s?$qt66$,
    $op66$[{"label":"A","text":"The 90 s ethanol exposure dissolved the cell's outer membrane, exposing thin peptidoglycan that could not retain crystal violet, so safranin stained the cell pink."},{"label":"B","text":"The prolonged ethanol drove additional crystal violet–iodine complex into the thick peptidoglycan, deepening the stain until it appeared pink rather than purple."},{"label":"C","text":"The extended decolorization removed the safranin counterstain from the cell, leaving the residual crystal violet to register as pink."},{"label":"D","text":"The prolonged ethanol washed the crystal violet–iodine complex out of the thick peptidoglycan, so the now-colorless cell took up safranin and appeared pink despite being gram-positive."}]$op66$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex66$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is D because over-decolorization extracts the trapped crystal violet–iodine complex even from a thick peptidoglycan wall, after which safranin counterstains the emptied cell pink and produces a false gram-negative result. In the Gram procedure, Gram's iodine acts as a mordant that complexes with crystal violet so that the dye "clump[s] and stay[s] contained in thick layers of peptidoglycan" (lines 3180–3182). Cells with thick peptidoglycan are "much less affected by the decolorizing agent; they generally retain the crystal violet dye and remain purple" (lines 3183–3185) — which is why the 10 s and 30 s trials stay purple. But the text warns that "if the decolorizer is left on a slide of pure gram-positive bacterial cells for too long, some of the gram-positive cells will be decolorized and stained red with safranin, suggesting a mixed culture" (lines 3204–3206). The 90 s trial is therefore an over-decolorization artifact, not a true change in the organism's wall chemistry. (Choice A) Gram-positive cells lack an outer membrane to begin with; the thin-peptidoglycan/outer-membrane architecture describes gram-negative cells, so dissolving a structure the cell does not have cannot be the cause. (Choice B) This reverses the chemistry: ethanol washes the crystal violet–iodine complex OUT, it does not drive more in, and excess complex would intensify purple, not yield pink. (Choice C) The counterstain is applied AFTER decolorization, so ethanol cannot remove safranin that is not yet on the slide, and crystal violet is purple rather than pink. (Choice D) Prolonged ethanol extracts the crystal violet–iodine complex from even the thick peptidoglycan, so the decolorized cell binds the safranin counterstain and reads pink despite truly being gram-positive — the over-decolorization artifact the text describes. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate the staining data across the three decolorization times with the retention mechanism of the crystal violet–iodine complex to identify over-decolorization as the artifact producing the false pink result.$ex66$,
    'hard', '2B', $cc66$Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)$cc66$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
